config ZMK_BEHAVIOR_TRACKBALL_CPI
    bool "Runtime trackball CPI behavior"
    default y
    depends on DT_HAS_ZMK_BEHAVIOR_TRACKBALL_CPI_ENABLED
    help
      Builds &tb_cpi, which retunes the PMW3610's CPI from a key. The dependency is
      generated from the compatible, so this stays off in any build whose devicetree
      has no such node.

config ZMK_BEHAVIOR_TRACKBALL_CPI_INIT_PRIORITY
    int "Runtime trackball CPI behavior init priority"
    default 90
    range 0 99
    depends on ZMK_BEHAVIOR_TRACKBALL_CPI
    help
      POST_KERNEL priority. The `device` phandle makes the sensor a devicetree
      dependency, and Zephyr's init-priority check rejects the build unless this
      behavior initializes after it -- the PMW3610 sits at INPUT_INIT_PRIORITY.
      Referencing the driver's own Kconfig symbol is not an option here: it does not
      exist in the left-half and dongle builds, which instantiate this behavior
      without a sensor. Hence a plain number, chosen to land well after any input
      device while staying inside POST_KERNEL.

config ZMK_INPUT_PROCESSOR_AUTO_LAYER
    bool "Self-maintaining auto-mouse layer processor"
    default y
    depends on DT_HAS_ZMK_INPUT_PROCESSOR_AUTO_LAYER_ENABLED
    help
      Builds &zip_auto_layer, the replacement for upstream's zip_temp_layer that reads
      the keymap to decide which presses belong to the layer rather than matching them
      against an excluded-positions list.

config ZMK_BEHAVIOR_BATTERY_REPORT
    bool "Type the split halves' battery percentages"
    default y
    depends on DT_HAS_ZMK_BEHAVIOR_BATTERY_REPORT_ENABLED
    help
      Builds &batt_rpt. Only useful on the central, which is where the peripheral
      battery levels are cached and where HID output happens, but it instantiates
      everywhere so one keymap builds for every shield.
